    "faq": {
      "type": "grs-pg-faq",
      "settings": {
        "loc": true
      },
      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why is the minimum railing height 42 inches on a California house?",
            "answer": "<p>The California code sets 42 inches as the minimum guard height for houses (CRC R312.1), where most states use 36 inches. Our 39.6-inch panel gives a 42-inch railing. The 46-inch panel, which gives 48 inches, is the common choice on a deck with a drop. Check with your local building department, and read our code page before ordering.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What does a California pool enclosure need, and which panel fits?",
            "answer": "<p>The Swimming Pool Safety Act (Health and Safety Code 115923) sets a barrier at least 60 inches high. Gaps must be under 4 inches, and the gate latch at least 60 inches above the ground. The 57.7-inch panel builds a 60-inch glass pool fence. Add gate hinges, a latch and the floor lock from our gate hardware. Your city or county confirms the details at permit time.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I use glass railing in a wildfire (WUI) zone?",
            "answer": "<p>Many hillside and foothill lots sit in a Wildland-Urban Interface zone, where the state sets rules for exterior building elements. Tempered glass and 2205 stainless steel do not burn, but the deck under them is usually wood. Check with your local fire authority and building department about deck construction and railing materials before you order.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do California earthquake rules change anything for the railing?",
            "answer": "<p>No. Seismic rules in the California code do not add railing requirements. What matters is the attachment: our engineering documents include testing and blocking details for the top-mount and side-mount spigots on concrete and on wood. Your building department may ask for them with the permit application; download them from the <a href=\"/pages/glass-railing-engineering-testing\">engineering page</a> and send them along.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q1",
        "q2",
        "q3",
        "q4"
      ]
    },
