    "rich": {
      "type": "grs-pg-rich",
      "blocks": {
        "cap": {
          "type": "caption",
          "settings": {
            "caption": "Glass railing in California"
          }
        },
        "h": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Frameless glass railings for California decks, pools and balconies"
          }
        },
        "t1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Where our California orders go</strong></p><p>Southern California is where the views are. Los Angeles, Orange County and San Diego homeowners put glass on hillside decks, ocean-view balconies and condo terraces. A railing without posts keeps that view open. In the Inland Empire and the Coachella Valley, including Palm Springs and Rancho Mirage, the typical project is a 60-inch glass pool fence. Summers there are hot and dry, and the pool is the yard.</p><p>In the Bay Area, San Francisco, the Peninsula, the East Bay and San Jose have steep lots with roof decks and rear decks. Fog and wind call for the 2205 stainless hardware. Sacramento and the Central Valley are pool-home country, with long, hot summers. Truckee and the Lake Tahoe basin get deep snow; the decks differ, but the railing is the same. The same panels and spigots work everywhere in the state; only the heights and the local permit change.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>The California code: 42-inch guards and 60-inch pool fences</strong></p><p>California uses its own code set, Title 24, with the 2022 California Residential Code and California Building Code. The California code sets a 42-inch minimum guard height even for single-family houses (CRC R312.1), where most other states use 36 inches. The 39.6-inch panel gives you exactly 42 inches. The 46-inch panel gives a 48-inch railing and is the common choice where there is a drop.</p><p>Pool enclosures fall under the Swimming Pool Safety Act (Health and Safety Code 115923). The barrier must be at least 60 inches high, with gaps under 4 inches and a latch at least 60 inches above ground. Our 57.7-inch panel makes a 60-inch glass pool fence. Earthquake rules add no railing requirement, and our engineering documents cover the spigot attachments. Read our <a href=\"/pages/california-building-standards-code-for-glass-railings\">California code page</a> and check with your local building department for the edition in force before you order.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Delivery to California from our warehouse</strong></p><p>Every order ships from our warehouse in Sainte-Julie, Quebec. Standard panels and hardware are processed in 2–3 business days, then delivered in 5–10 business days anywhere in the continental United States. A freight carrier brings the crate to your address with liftgate service. Shipping is free on orders over 3,000 USD. The price at checkout is final: no duties, tariffs or hidden fees, even though the order crosses the border.</p><p>Custom glass takes 2–3 business days of processing plus 2–3 weeks of shipping, so measure early for a summer deck. When the crate arrives, inspect every panel. Report any damage within 3 days of delivery and before installation, and we replace or refund the item at no cost. Glass is made to order and final sale; unused hardware in its original packaging can be exchanged for store credit within 30 days. See the <a href=\"/pages/free-shipping-information\">shipping details</a> for the fine print.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
