    "cols": {
      "type": "grs-pg-cols",
      "blocks": {
        "c1": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Ocean-view and hillside decks",
            "text": "<p>From Laguna Beach bluffs to the Berkeley hills, decks are built for the view. A frameless glass deck railing has no posts in the sightline. Each 12 mm panel sits in two spigots, top-mounted on the deck or side-mounted on the fascia. Add the square caprail for a top rail.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c2": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Pool fences and pool decks",
            "text": "<p>From Palm Springs to Sacramento, backyard pools need a 60-inch enclosure under the state pool safety act. The 57.7-inch panel, gate hinges, a latch set high and the floor lock build a glass pool fence. The pool stays in view from the house.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c3": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Condo balconies and rooftop terraces",
            "text": "<p>Condo balconies in San Diego, Los Angeles and San Francisco take 42-inch or 48-inch glass balcony railings. Rooftop terraces use the same panels with wall connectors at the parapet. Ask your HOA or condo board about finish and height before ordering; a sample kit helps with the approval.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
